# The card's radios come up BLOCKED on every boot — asserted here, not
# inherited. This is a separate rules file from 99-dashberry.rules on
# purpose: it is the one rule firstinstall removes, on the single build
# that is meant to boot live (a debug card given a --wifi profile).
#
# Defense in depth, NOT the whole defense (learned 2026-08-05, the hard
# way). Current Pi OS images ship rfkill_default.conf (`options rfkill
# default_state=0`, raspberrypi-sys-mods 20241030+1), so the kernel
# already registers every switch soft-blocked and this write is usually
# 1-over-1; on an older image — or if that conf ever goes away — it is
# what blocks a JOIN WIFI card (RF_JOIN=1, regdom kept, so nothing
# hard-blocks wlan). Either way the add-time state is only the FIRST word
# of a boot: systemd-rfkill's ADD-handler restore and NetworkManager's
# persisted WirelessEnabled are both written over the switch AFTERWARDS,
# each replaying a /var/lib state file that on a production card is a
# frozen copy of install-time state, served identically every boot. A card
# whose frozen state said "unblocked" therefore booted RF-ENABLED despite
# this rule. What actually holds the boot RF-KILLED is firstinstall:
# radios_off pins both state files to "blocked" before the overlay
# freezes them, and a production card masks systemd-rfkill outright.
#
# ACTION=="add" is load-bearing: an rfkill state change also reaches udev,
# and an unqualified rule would re-block the radios underneath a live
# JOIN WIFI session the instant rf-ctl unblocked them. Only the switch
# APPEARING (boot, or a driver rebind) may force it down; rf-ctl's
# explicit `rfkill unblock wifi` during a session is untouched.
#
# wlan only. The Bluetooth switch is a separate rfkill device and is not
# in rev 8's scope — the panel's glyph reads type wlan and nothing else.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="rfkill", ATTR{type}=="wlan", ATTR{soft}="1"
